      "correct": 1,
      "explanation": "Correct: (B) Weight, normal force, and tension toward the peg. A free-body diagram shows only real forces exerted by identifiable objects: Earth pulls down (weight), the table pushes up (normal force), and the string pulls toward the peg (tension). Vertically, the normal force balances the weight. Horizontally, tension is the only force, so tension alone is the net force, and it supplies the centripetal acceleration: T = mv²/r. \"Centripetal\" names the direction of the net force; it is not an extra force with its own arrow.\n\n(A) is wrong because the centripetal force is not a separate force; here the tension itself is the inward net force, so adding another arrow double-counts it.\n(C) is wrong because no object exerts an outward centrifugal force on the puck; if the string broke, the puck would move off along a tangent line, not straight outward.\n(D) is wrong because no object pushes the puck forward; on a frictionless table it keeps moving by inertia, and only the inward tension is needed to change its direction.",
