      "correct": 3,
      "explanation": "Correct: (D) It becomes four times as large. The net force toward the center must equal mv²/r. With m and r fixed, the required force is proportional to v². Doubling the speed multiplies v² by 2² = 4, so the net inward force, here supplied by the string's tension, must become four times as large. This square dependence is why tight, fast turns demand so much more friction or tension than slow ones, and why strings snap when the speed is pushed up.\n\n(A) is wrong because the required inward force depends on speed through mv²/r, so a faster ball needs a larger net force to bend its path into the same circle.\n(B) is wrong because doubling would be right only if the force were proportional to v, but it is proportional to v², so the factor is 2² = 4.\n(C) is wrong because a larger speed needs more inward force, not less; halving the force would let the ball move on a wider circle, not the same one.",
