      "correct": 1,
      "explanation": "Correct: (B) 13 m/s. Static friction is the only horizontal force, so it supplies the centripetal acceleration. At the maximum speed it is at its limit, μ<sub>s</sub>F<sub>N</sub> = μ<sub>s</sub>mg. Setting μ<sub>s</sub>mg = mv²/r, the mass cancels, giving v = √(μ<sub>s</sub>gr) = √((0.60)(9.8 m/s²)(30 m)) = √176 ≈ 13 m/s. Faster than this, friction cannot provide mv²/r and the car slides toward the outside of the curve.\n\n(A) is wrong because 4.2 m/s is √(μ<sub>s</sub>r) = √18, which leaves out g; the friction force depends on the normal force mg, so g must appear.\n(C) is wrong because 17 m/s is √(gr) = √294, which leaves out the coefficient of friction, as if the road could supply a friction force equal to the full weight.\n(D) is wrong because m appears in both the maximum friction force μ<sub>s</sub>mg and the required mv²/r, so it cancels and the maximum speed is the same for any car.",
