      "correct": 0,
      "explanation": "Correct: (A) Down the slope of the bank, toward the inside of the curve. At the design speed, the horizontal component of the normal force alone supplies mv²/r. Faster than that, the car needs a larger inward net force than the normal force can give, and without friction it would slide up and outward. Static friction opposes that tendency to slip, so it points down the slope. Its horizontal component adds to the inward force. The CED limits banked curves with friction to this kind of qualitative reasoning.\n\n(B) is wrong because friction points up the slope only when the car goes slower than the design speed and would otherwise slide down toward the inside.\n(C) is wrong because a car moving at constant speed needs no friction along its direction of travel; the friction that matters on the curve acts across the direction of motion, along the bank.\n(D) is wrong because the bank removes the need for friction only at the design speed; at any other speed static friction must make up the difference in the inward force.",
