      "prompt": "A planet of unknown mass M has several small moons, each moving in a nearly circular orbit of radius r (measured from the planet's center) with period T. Each moon's mass is much smaller than M. Use G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg². (a) Starting from Newton's law of gravitation and Newton's second law, derive an expression for the orbital speed v of a moon in terms of G, M, and r. (b) Derive an expression for the orbital period T in terms of G, M, and r, and use it to determine which quantities should be graphed to give a straight line whose slope can be used to find M. (c) Astronomers measure the following data for five moons. Orbital radius r (× 10⁸ m): 1.85, 2.38, 2.95, 3.77, 5.27. Period T (× 10⁵ s): 0.81, 1.18, 1.63, 2.35, 3.88. Add any needed columns to the table, plot the graph from part (b), and draw a best-fit line. (d) Use the slope of your best-fit line to calculate the mass of the planet. (e) A student claims that if a second moon with ten times the mass of the innermost moon were placed in an orbit of the same radius, the planet's stronger pull would give it a shorter period. Evaluate this claim.",
