      "prompt": "A 0.50 kg cart starts at rest at t = 0 on a level, low-friction track; take rightward as positive. A fan attached to the cart and, later, a hand exert a horizontal net force on the cart that varies as follows. From t = 0 to t = 2.0 s, the net force is a constant +0.40 N. From t = 2.0 s to t = 3.0 s, the net force is zero. From t = 3.0 s to t = 5.0 s, the net force changes steadily (linearly with time) from 0 at t = 3.0 s to −0.60 N at t = 5.0 s. (a) Sketch a graph of the cart's momentum p versus time t from t = 0 to t = 5.0 s. Label the values of p at t = 2.0 s, 3.0 s, and 5.0 s, and describe the shape of each segment. (b) Calculate the cart's velocity at t = 5.0 s, and state its direction. (c) A student wants to verify experimentally that the impulse exerted on a cart equals its change in momentum. The student has the cart, a balance, a force sensor that can be mounted on the cart with a spring bumper on its front, a motion sensor, and a fixed wall at the end of the track. Describe a procedure, including how each measurement is made and how the data would be analyzed to test the relationship. (d) In one trial the cart (total mass 0.50 kg with the sensor) moves toward the wall at 0.60 m/s and rebounds at 0.45 m/s. The force sensor shows the wall's force on the cart rising from zero to a maximum of 20.5 N and falling back to zero in an approximately triangular shape that lasts 0.050 s. Calculate the impulse and the change in momentum, and state whether this trial supports the impulse-momentum theorem.",
