      "title": "A Dart That Sticks Versus a Dart That Bounces",
      "prompt": "A 0.050 kg dart moving horizontally at 8.0 m/s strikes a 0.45 kg cart that is initially at rest on a level, frictionless track; take the dart's initial direction as positive. In Trial 1 the dart has a sticky tip and sticks to the cart. In Trial 2 the dart has a rubber tip and bounces straight back at 2.0 m/s. (a) Draw momentum bar charts for Trial 1 and for Trial 2, each showing the momentum of the dart, of the cart, and of the dart-cart system before and after the collision. Use a scale of one unit = 0.10 kg·m/s and show negative momentum as a bar below the zero line. (b) Calculate the velocity of the cart after the collision in each trial. (c) In which trial does the cart end up moving faster? Justify your answer using the impulse each object exerts on the other, without relying only on your numbers from part (b). (d) A student claims that because the dart in Trial 2 reverses direction, more kinetic energy must be lost in Trial 2 than in Trial 1. Evaluate this claim with calculations.",
