      "prompt": "A student wants to find the mass M of a uniform meterstick without putting it on a balance. The student has the meterstick, a knife-edge pivot on which the stick can be balanced at any point, a set of known masses with light clips, and a balance that may be used only for the known masses. (a) Describe a procedure the student could use to collect data from which M can be determined, including how the stick's center of mass is located. (b) For one trial, a known mass m hangs from the 5.0 cm mark and the stick balances on the pivot at position p (in cm). Draw a force diagram of the meterstick, showing each force as an arrow starting at the point where it is exerted. Then use torques about the pivot to write an equation relating m, M, and p (the stick's center of mass is at the 50.0 cm mark), and determine which quantities should be graphed to give a straight line whose slope is M. (c) The student hangs each known mass from the 5.0 cm mark and records the balance point. Known mass m (kg): 0.050, 0.100, 0.150, 0.200, 0.250. Balance point p (cm): 36.8, 29.5, 25.1, 21.8, 19.6. Add any needed columns, plot your graph from part (b), draw a best-fit line, and calculate M. (d) Calculate the force exerted by the pivot on the stick in the trial with the 0.150 kg mass, and explain why it is not equal to the weight of the stick alone.",
