            "The hoop's mass is all at the rim, so its rotational inertia (MR²) is larger than the disk's (½MR²). For the same speed, it needs more rotational kinetic energy.",
            "So a larger share of the hoop's kinetic energy goes into rotation, leaving a smaller share as translational kinetic energy.",
            "Smaller translational kinetic energy at the same mass means a smaller center-of-mass speed at every height along the incline.",
            "Since the disk is moving faster at every point along the same path, it covers the incline in less time: the disk reaches the bottom first. (Simply stating 'energy is conserved' does not explain the difference, because energy is conserved for both.)"
