    {
      "id": "frq-6-6",
      "source": "Original",
      "title": "Escape Speed and the Energy of Orbits",
      "prompt": "A 500 kg probe is near Mars, which has mass M = 6.42 × 10²³ kg and radius R = 3.39 × 10⁶ m. Ignore Mars's atmosphere and rotation, and use G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg². The probe-Mars system has gravitational potential energy U_g = −GMm/r, where r is the distance from Mars's center. (a) Starting from conservation of energy, derive an expression for the escape speed v_esc from a distance r from the center of a planet of mass M. (b) Derive an expression for the speed v_orb of a circular orbit at the same distance r. Find the ratio v_esc/v_orb, and calculate both speeds at Mars's surface. (c) Sketch a graph of the gravitational potential energy U_g of the probe-Mars system as a function of r, from r = R to r = 5R. Label the value at r = R, and describe the shape of the graph. (d) A student claims that since a satellite in a higher circular orbit moves more slowly, it has less total mechanical energy, so moving the probe from a low orbit to a higher one requires taking energy away from it. Evaluate this claim. Support your answer by calculating the total mechanical energy of the system for circular orbits at r₁ = 3.79 × 10⁶ m (400 km above the surface) and r₂ = 2R.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "label": "(a)",
          "ask": "Derive the escape speed.",
          "steps": [
            "Take the probe-Mars system. With gravity as the only force, which is internal to the system, mechanical energy is conserved: E = ½mv² − GMm/r is constant.",
            "To escape, the probe must reach an infinite separation (where U_g = 0). The minimum launch speed is the one for which the probe arrives there with zero speed (K = 0), so its total energy is E = 0 + 0 = 0.",
            "Since E is constant, the launch must also have E = 0: ½m v_esc² − GMm/r = 0.",
            "The probe's mass cancels: v_esc² = 2GM/r, so v_esc = √(2GM/r).",
            "Any total energy of zero or more lets the probe escape; a negative total energy means the system is bound and the probe eventually falls back or stays in orbit."
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "(b)",
          "ask": "Derive the circular-orbit speed, find the ratio, and calculate both speeds.",
          "steps": [
            "In a circular orbit, gravity supplies the inward net force: GMm/r² = mv²/r, so v_orb = √(GM/r).",
            "Ratio: v_esc/v_orb = √(2GM/r)/√(GM/r) = √2 ≈ 1.41, independent of M and r.",
            "At the surface (r = R): GM/R = (6.67 × 10⁻¹¹)(6.42 × 10²³)/(3.39 × 10⁶) = 4.28 × 10¹³/3.39 × 10⁶ ≈ 1.26 × 10⁷ m²/s².",
            "v_orb = √(1.26 × 10⁷) ≈ 3.55 × 10³ m/s (3.6 km/s).",
            "v_esc = √(2 × 1.26 × 10⁷) ≈ 5.03 × 10³ m/s (5.0 km/s), which is √2 times v_orb."
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "(c)",
          "ask": "Sketch U_g versus r.",
          "steps": [
            "At r = R: U_g = −GMm/R = −(6.67 × 10⁻¹¹)(6.42 × 10²³)(500)/(3.39 × 10⁶) = −(2.14 × 10¹⁶)/(3.39 × 10⁶) ≈ −6.3 × 10⁹ J.",
            "Correct sketch: the whole curve lies BELOW the r-axis (U_g is negative everywhere). It starts at about −6.3 × 10⁹ J at r = R and rises toward zero as r increases, approaching the r-axis but never reaching or crossing it.",
            "The curve is steepest near the planet and flattens out with distance (concave down): it is proportional to −1/r, so U_g is −3.2 × 10⁹ J at 2R, −2.1 × 10⁹ J at 3R, and −1.3 × 10⁹ J at 5R.",
            "Its slope at any r equals the magnitude of the gravitational force GMm/r², which also gets smaller as r increases. Increasing r always increases U_g (makes it less negative), because separating attracting objects requires positive work."
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "(d)",
          "ask": "Evaluate the claim about orbital energy.",
          "steps": [
            "In a circular orbit, v² = GM/r, so K = ½mv² = GMm/(2r), and E = K + U_g = GMm/(2r) − GMm/r = −GMm/(2r).",
            "GMm = (6.67 × 10⁻¹¹)(6.42 × 10²³)(500) ≈ 2.14 × 10¹⁶ J·m.",
            "Low orbit: E₁ = −(2.14 × 10¹⁶)/(2 × 3.79 × 10⁶) ≈ −2.8 × 10⁹ J.",
            "High orbit (r₂ = 2R = 6.78 × 10⁶ m): E₂ = −(2.14 × 10¹⁶)/(2 × 6.78 × 10⁶) ≈ −1.6 × 10⁹ J.",
            "E₂ is less negative, so it is GREATER than E₁: raising the orbit requires ADDING energy, E₂ − E₁ ≈ (−1.58 × 10⁹ J) − (−2.82 × 10⁹ J) ≈ 1.2 × 10⁹ J (for example, positive work done by the probe's rocket engine). The claim is incorrect.",
            "The student is right that the higher orbit is slower (K is smaller), but the gravitational potential energy rises by twice as much as K falls, because U_g = −2K in a circular orbit. So the total energy increases with r."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "added": "2026-11-28"
    }
