      "title": "Finding a Spring Constant from Oscillation Periods",
      "prompt": "A student hangs a spring vertically and attaches different masses m to its lower end. For each mass, the student pulls the mass down a small distance, releases it, and uses a stopwatch to time 10 complete oscillations. Hanging mass m (kg): 0.100, 0.200, 0.300, 0.400, 0.500. Time for 10 oscillations (s): 4.66, 6.42, 7.82, 9.02, 10.04. (a) Explain why the student times 10 oscillations instead of one. (b) Determine which quantities should be graphed to produce a straight line, add any needed columns to the table, plot the graph, and draw a best-fit line. (c) Use the slope of the best-fit line to calculate the spring constant k. Then explain what the nonzero vertical intercept of the line suggests about the spring. (d) A 0.20 kg block on a spring with k = 20 N/m on a horizontal frictionless surface is pulled to x = +0.050 m and released from rest at t = 0, so its position is x(t) = A cos(2πt/T). Calculate the period, the maximum speed, and the maximum acceleration. Then sketch position versus time, velocity versus time, and acceleration versus time for two full periods, on three separate graphs stacked vertically so that they share the same time axis, labeling the period and the maximum value on each.",
